25: PROVIDE MAINTENANCE, SUPPORT, UPDATES, ENHANCEMENTS, OR MODIFICATIONS. 1: # Quickstart
 2: 1. Clone submodules:
 3:     ```bash
 4:     git submodule update --init --recursive
 5:     ```
 6: 1. Build the tests in this repo:
 7:     ```bash
 8:     ./build.sh
 9:     ```
10: 
11: The tests' binaries will be installed in `build/`. The tests whose source code can be found in `bareMetalC/` will be installed in `build/bareMetalC/`, the tests in `imagenet/` will be installed in `build/imagenet/`, and so forth.
12: 
13: To run the tests yourself on a Gemmini ISA simulator, follow these steps:
14: 1. Install [esp-isa-sim](https://github.com/ucb-bar/esp-isa-sim). If you are using [Chipyard](https://github.com/ucb-bar/chipyard), then running `./scripts/build-toolchains.sh esp-tools` from Chipyard's root directory will install it for you. The ISA simulator is called `spike`.
15: 1. Run test programs on `spike`:
16:     ```bash
17:     cd build/bareMetalC
18:     spike --extension=gemmini mvin_mvout-baremetal
19:     ```
20: 
21: # Writing Your Own Gemmini Tests
22: `bareMetalC/template.c` is a template Gemmini test that you can base your own Gemmini tests off of. To write your own Gemmini test, run:
23: 
24: ```bash
25: cp bareMetalC/template.c bareMetalC/my_test.c
26: ```
27: 
28: Then, add `my_test` to the `tests` list at the top of `bareMetalC/Makefile`. Afterwards, running `./build.sh` will install `my_test-baremetal` in `build/bareMetalC`.
